course: dictionary
lesson: 8134
title: Replete: full and well supplied
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Replete is a formal word, the kind you meet in old stories and tests. It means full of something, or well supplied with it. «The castle larder was replete with bread, cheese and honey.» The larder had plenty of food in it, so replete means full of.
explain: Replete can also mean very full after eating. «After the feast, the guests sat back, replete.» They had eaten so much they could not eat another bite. In everyday talk, people usually say «full of» or «stuffed» instead.
explain: Check the words around replete. In «a story replete with adventure», the words that follow tell you what the story is full of. Swap in full of: «a story full of adventure» still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Replete means full of something, or well supplied with it.
fact: Replete can also mean very full after eating.
fact: The phrases to look out for are «replete with» and «feeling replete».
fact: «Replete» is a formal word: writers use it in books and tests, while people in everyday talk say «full of».
fact: «Repletes» are honeypot ants whose bellies stretch full of sweet liquid; Aboriginal people in Australia have long dug them up as a sweet treat.
exercise: «The shelves were replete with books.» What does replete mean here? | Very cold | *Full of, well supplied with | Hidden under dust | Empty and bare
exercise: After a huge roast dinner, Sam says, «I feel replete.» What does he mean? | *He is very full after eating | He is very hungry | He is angry with his dinner | He is tired from running
exercise: Which phrase means the same as «a story replete with adventure»? | *A story full of adventure | A story about a sad dog | A story with no adventure at all | A story told in a whisper
test: «The garden was replete with roses and bees in the summer.» What is replete closest to? | *Full of | Grey with | Hidden behind | Empty of
test: Why might a writer choose replete instead of full? | It is a Scots word for wet | It means the opposite of full | It is a playground slang word | *It is a more formal word, used in books and tests
exam: Which meaning of replete is correct? | *Full of something, or very full after eating | Quiet and shy | Fast and loud | Cold and empty
